Z||000026||Cliff Pennock||03-15-2001||06:55 PM||||Voytek, you are right and to be honest, I don't have a problem with that. It all has to do with people's preferences. Most of the members here:<P>A) Are men, therefore love women<BR>B) Love Fiero's<BR>C) Are men, therefore love women<P>Now the love for Fiero's was aquired. The love for women is genetic. In other words, we men are perfectly capable of ignoring something Fiero-related. But we can't ignore a woman. Don't blame us, it's been genetically programmed. Let me put it another way. You're a man and you have a question about your Fiero that nobody has an answer to. Result? Nobody answers. Now, you're a woman and you have a question about your Fiero that nobody has an answers to. Result? Ten posts that will say "I have no idea, but thank you for asking", 23 posts that will ask "where do you live? Maybe I can drop by and help you", 36 marriage proposals (woman+fiero=need I explain?), 42 posts that will say "I need more info to answer, like 'are you single?'". Again, it's genetic.<P>If - as a man - you want to get another man's attention, you need to rephrase your question. Your original question "Clutch Noise - Need help" scores very low on a man's attention scale. Next time, try "Clutch Noise - can't pick up babes because of it", or "My girlfriend says she heard a clutch coise". You could also try the semi-accidental misspelling: "Clutch Nose" (hey, it's a bodypart - we men get our hopes up easily) and I'm sure you can think of a few misspellings for "Clutch" that would get our attention.<P>Now if everything fails, change your screenname in something more feminime (and leave us in the dark concerning your true gender) like "Hottie Voytek" (proven to work), "erika" (note here the all lowercase, this way it could also stand for Erik A) or "Fierotica".<P>It's really all quite simple.||||reg||1
